<Layout title="How Much Should You Tip? | Tip Calculator" description="A practical guide to tipping for restaurants, takeout, delivery, bars, rides, hotels, and hair services."><ContentPage title="How much should you tip?" intro="Tipping depends on the service, local custom, and your own budget. Use these ranges as context, not compulsory rules."><h2>Common U.S. situations</h2><h3>Full-service restaurants</h3><p>For satisfactory table service, 15–20% is a customary range; 20% is a common choice for good service. Check whether a service charge is already included.</p><h3>Takeout and food delivery</h3><p>Takeout tipping is optional. Delivery tips are common because the driver spends time travelling and handling your order. Distance, weather, order size, and service can guide the amount.</p><h3>Bars, rides, and personal services</h3><p>At a bar, a small per-drink tip is common; taxi and rideshare tipping is common but discretionary. For hair services, 15–20% is a common range. Hotel tipping varies by role and the help provided.</p><h2>When the bill includes a service charge</h2><p>Read the wording carefully. A service charge or gratuity may already cover tipping, while a general fee may not. Ask the staff if you are unsure, then use the calculator with the amount you actually want to tip.</p></ContentPage></Layout>
